R = "[Use your final result here]"
SURV = [
# ---------------- Basic
("Basic", "What is survival analysis?",
 '"Thank you. Survival analysis studies the time until an event happens, here death. Its key feature is that it can use patients whose follow-up ends before the event, called censored patients, instead of throwing them away or treating them as survivors."',
 "Clear and simple.",
 'You will know this. Keep it to two sentences.'),
("Basic", "Why did you use survival analysis instead of predicting death as yes or no?",
 '"A yes-or-no outcome ignores timing: a patient who dies on day 2 and one who dies on day 300 count the same. Survival analysis uses when the event happened and handles patients whose follow-up ended early. It also gives risk at any time point, so I can report survival at 28 days, 90 days and one year from one model. My supervisor recommended this change, and it fits the clinical question better."',
 "Confident. This is central to your thesis.",
 '"The core reason is that timing carries information a yes-or-no outcome throws away."'),
("Basic", "What is a non-communicable disease, and which ones did you include?",
 '"NCDs are long-term conditions not passed from person to person. Following the WHO, I included the four main groups: cardiovascular diseases, cancers, chronic respiratory diseases and diabetes."',
 "Plain.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "Why only those four groups?",
 '"They are the four groups the WHO uses to track NCDs, and together they cause most NCD deaths: at least 19 million from cardiovascular disease in 2021, 10 million from cancer, 4 million from chronic respiratory disease and over 2 million from diabetes. Using the WHO definition makes the study comparable with global statistics."',
 "Factual.",
 '"Other chronic conditions, such as chronic kidney or liver disease, matter too, but I kept to the standard WHO definition."'),
("Basic", "What is MIMIC-IV?",
 '"MIMIC-IV is a freely accessible, de-identified database from the electronic health record of the Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center in Boston. It contains hospital and ICU data such as measurements, laboratory results, diagnoses, procedures and treatments. I used version 3.1."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "How did you get access to MIMIC-IV?",
 '"Through PhysioNet. I completed the required human research training, became a credentialed user and signed the data use agreement. After download, I checked every file against the official SHA256 checksums."',
 "Simple.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "How many patients were in your study, and how many had an NCD?",
 '"65,283 adult first ICU stays with recorded vital signs. Of these, 30,467, or 46.7%, had an NCD as the principal diagnosis: 24,848 cardiovascular, 4,192 cancer, 963 diabetes and 464 chronic respiratory. Under the wider sensitivity definition, 40,733 patients had an NCD."',
 "Precise. Know these numbers by heart.",
 '"May I check the cohort table so I give the exact numbers?"'),
("Basic", "Why only the first ICU stay?",
 '"Using one stay per patient keeps observations independent, so no patient counts twice. It also stops the same patient appearing in both training and test data. The first stay is the natural choice because later stays depend on what happened in earlier ones."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "Why only adults?",
 '"Children have different physiology, normal ranges and causes of ICU admission, and the four NCD groups are mainly adult conditions. I included patients aged 18 and over at ICU admission."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "Why did you exclude stays without recorded vital signs?",
 '"Without any vital signs in the first 24 hours, there is almost nothing to predict from, and such stays are often data errors or very short stays. Excluding them keeps the input data meaningful."',
 "Brief.",
 '"I can give the exact number excluded from the pipeline log."'),
("Basic", "What is your primary outcome?",
 '"Survival time up to one year: the number of days to death, censored at 365 days for patients not known to have died by then. In the data it is two columns, survival_days and the event indicator died_1yr."',
 "Precise.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "Why a one-year horizon?",
 '"Many deaths after ICU happen after discharge, so in-hospital death misses part of the picture. One year captures the longer effect of critical illness, especially for chronic disease. It also matches the follow-up that MIMIC-IV provides through linked death records."',
 "Reasoned.",
 '"Other horizons are possible; I also report 28-day and 90-day performance."'),
("Basic", "What is censoring?",
 '"Censoring means we do not know the exact event time for a patient, only that they were event-free up to a certain time. In my study, patients not known to have died within a year are censored at 365 days."',
 "Simple.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What type of censoring is in your data?",
 '"Right censoring, almost all of it administrative: every patient not known to have died is censored at exactly 365 days. That is the simplest kind, because the censoring time is set by the study design, not by patient characteristics."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is your secondary outcome, and why did you include it?",
 '"Discharge home alive, among patients who survive to hospital discharge. Survival is not the only thing that matters; whether a patient goes home reflects recovery and affects discharge planning. I use it to test whether the ranking of the three approaches holds for a different kind of outcome."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What are the survival function and the hazard function?",
 '"The survival function gives the probability of still being alive at time t. The hazard function is the instantaneous risk of dying at time t, given the patient has survived until then. They are two views of the same thing; one can be calculated from the other."',
 "Precise.",
 '"In plain words, survival is the chance of being alive; hazard is the risk right now."'),
("Basic", "What is a Kaplan-Meier curve, and what did yours show?",
 '"It is a non-parametric estimate of the survival function that accounts for censoring. My curves showed that NCD patients as a whole had slightly better one-year survival than non-NCD patients, 22.8% against 27.0% died. Within NCD groups, cancer patients did worst, with 46.7% dying within a year, and diabetes patients did best at 13.1%."',
 "Clear and numerate.",
 '"The curves are in the EDA chapter; may I point to the figure?"'),
("Basic", "What is a log-rank test, and what did it show?",
 '"The log-rank test compares survival curves between groups. Each NCD group differed significantly from non-NCD patients: cardiovascular, cancer and diabetes with p below 0.001, and chronic respiratory with p of 0.006. The direction differed: cancer did worse, the others did better."',
 "Factual.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is the Cox proportional hazards model, in simple terms?",
 '"It models a patient\'s hazard as a baseline hazard over time multiplied by a factor that depends on their predictors. It does not need the baseline to have a particular shape, which made it the standard survival regression method after Cox introduced it in 1972."',
 "Plain.",
 '"The key idea is that predictors multiply the risk up or down."'),
("Basic", "What is a hazard ratio?",
 '"A hazard ratio compares hazards between two groups or for a one-unit change in a predictor. A hazard ratio of 1.5 means a 50% higher instantaneous risk of death, assuming proportional hazards."',
 "Simple.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is a random survival forest?",
 '"It is the random forest adapted to censored data, introduced by Ishwaran and colleagues in 2008. Each tree is grown with survival splitting rules, and the forest combines the trees to give each patient a survival curve. It captures non-linear effects and interactions without having to specify them."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "How does XGBoost handle survival data?",
 '"XGBoost has survival objectives built in. The accelerated failure time objective, described by Barnwal and colleagues, models the logarithm of survival time and handles censored observations directly. XGBoost also has a Cox-type objective. I used [state which one], because [reason]."',
 "Precise. Know which objective you used.",
 '"I would need to check the exact loss settings, but the objective is named in the code and in Chapter 3."'),
("Basic", "What is the general approach?",
 '"The general model is trained on all ICU patients in the training data: non-NCD patients and the NCD training patients together. It is then tested on the held-out group of NCD patients."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is the disease-specific approach?",
 '"The disease-specific model is trained only on NCD patients in the training data, then tested on the same held-out group of NCD patients."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is transfer learning in your study?",
 '"Transfer learning starts from the general model and adjusts it using NCD training patients. The idea is to keep what the general model learnt from the large population while fitting the NCD group more closely."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is the held-out group of NCD patients, and why is it the same for every model?",
 '"It is a random 20% of NCD patients, stratified by the outcome, kept aside from all training and tuning. Every model, under every approach and method, is tested on exactly these patients. That is what makes the comparison fair: differences in results come from the approach, not from different test patients."',
 "Firm. This is the core of your design.",
 'Short answer; never hesitate on this one.'),
("Basic", "What is the C-index?",
 '"The concordance index is the share of comparable patient pairs where the model gives higher risk to the patient who died earlier. 0.5 is random, 1.0 is perfect. It is the survival version of the AUC."',
 "Simple.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is time-dependent AUC, and why 28 days, 90 days and one year?",
 '"Time-dependent AUC measures how well the model separates patients who die by a given time from those still alive at that time. I chose 28 days, 90 days and one year because they are standard clinical time points: short-term, medium-term and the full horizon of the study."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "What is the integrated Brier score?",
 '"It is the Brier score, the squared error between predicted survival probability and actual status, averaged over the follow-up period, with weights to handle censoring. Lower is better, and it reflects both discrimination and calibration."',
 "Precise.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Basic", "Why did you use predictors from the first 24 hours only?",
 '"The first 24 hours is a common and practical prediction point: enough data has been collected to judge how sick the patient is, and decisions about care are still being made. It also prevents later information, which could reflect the outcome, from leaking into the model."',
 "Reasoned.",
 'Short answer.'),
# ---------------- Intermediate
("Intermediate", "How exactly did you identify NCD patients from diagnosis codes?",
 '"MIMIC-IV uses both ICD-9 and ICD-10. I mapped both. For ICD-9: 390 to 459 for cardiovascular, 140 to 208 for cancer, 490 to 496 for chronic respiratory and 250 for diabetes. For ICD-10: I codes for cardiovascular, C codes for cancer, J40 to J47 for chronic respiratory, and E08 to E11 and E13 for diabetes."',
 "Precise. Examiners may test one code range.",
 '"The exact mapping is in the code and the appendix; I can show it."'),
("Intermediate", "What is the principal diagnosis in MIMIC-IV, and how reliable is it?",
 '"It is the diagnosis with sequence number 1 for the hospital admission, assigned by coders for billing. It usually reflects the main reason for admission, but it is not always the reason for ICU admission. That is a known limitation, which is why I ran a sensitivity analysis using the first three diagnoses."',
 "Honest.",
 '"Coding accuracy cannot be checked against charts in de-identified data, so I treat it as a limitation."'),
("Intermediate", "Why use principal diagnosis in the main analysis and the first three in the sensitivity analysis?",
 '"The principal diagnosis is the strictest definition: the NCD is the main problem. The first three diagnoses capture patients where an NCD is important but not first. If the ranking of approaches is the same under both, the conclusion does not depend on how strictly NCD is defined."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Diagnosis codes are assigned at discharge. Is that not leakage?",
 '"Thank you, that is an important point. The codes define which group a patient belongs to; they are never used as predictors. So the model itself only uses the first 24 hours. In practice, a clinician would usually know at admission whether a patient has a major NCD, so the group is a reasonable proxy for what is known early."',
 "Calm and direct. Do not get defensive.",
 '"The codes select patients but never enter the model; I accept that real-time group assignment is approximate."'),
("Intermediate", "How were deaths after hospital discharge captured?",
 '"MIMIC-IV fills the date of death from hospital records and from linked state death records, so deaths after discharge are included, up to a follow-up limit after the last hospital discharge, which the MIMIC-IV documentation sets at one year. That is why a one-year horizon fits the data."',
 "Precise. Check the documentation before the defence.",
 '"I would like to confirm the exact follow-up rule in the MIMIC-IV documentation rather than guess."'),
("Intermediate", "Are patients without a death record really alive at one year?",
 '"Not certainly. They are not known to have died, so I treat them as censored at 365 days, not as known survivors. Some deaths, for example outside the state, may be missed, which could slightly underestimate mortality. That would affect all three approaches equally."',
 "Honest.",
 '"I treated them as censored, which is the standard approach given the data."'),
("Intermediate", "Cancer patients had very different survival. How did that affect your models?",
 '"Yes, 46.7% of cancer patients died within a year, against 19.1% of cardiovascular patients. Because the disease-specific and transfer learning models pool all four NCD groups, I reported the C-index and calibration separately for each group, to check that overall performance does not hide poor results for cancer patients. [State what the per-group results showed.]"',
 "Engaged. This shows you thought about it in advance.",
 '"The per-group results are in Chapter 4; I can turn to them."'),
("Intermediate", "Chronic respiratory disease and diabetes groups are small. How did you handle that?",
 '"With 464 and 963 patients, I did not build separate models for them. They are part of the pooled NCD group. I report their results separately, but with wide confidence intervals, so I draw only cautious conclusions about them individually."',
 "Honest.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Why did NCD patients have lower mortality than non-NCD patients?",
 '"The NCD group is dominated by cardiovascular patients, 24,848 of 30,467, and many of them are admitted after planned cardiac procedures, who tend to do well. Non-NCD patients include sepsis, trauma and other acute illnesses with high mortality. Cancer patients, by contrast, did much worse than non-NCD patients."',
 "Thoughtful. Present the cardiac surgery point as a likely explanation, not a proven one.",
 '"That is my interpretation; I did not separate planned from emergency admissions, so I would present it as a likely reason."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you check the proportional hazards assumption?",
 '"I used Schoenfeld residuals for each fitted Cox model, with a test for each predictor and a global test. If residuals show a trend over time, the effect of that predictor is not constant. I report the results alongside the main findings."',
 "Precise.",
 '"The test results are in the appendix."'),
("Intermediate", "What if the proportional hazards assumption was violated?",
 '"With large samples, small violations are almost always detected. For prediction, a mild violation mainly affects calibration over time, and the C-index is often still reasonable. Options include stratifying by the predictor or adding a time interaction. The random survival forest and XGBoost do not rely on this assumption, which is one reason to include them. [State what you found.]"',
 "Balanced.",
 '"I report the violations openly; the non-Cox methods provide a check."'),
("Intermediate", "Why no transfer learning for the random survival forest?",
 '"There is no standard way to continue training an already fitted random forest, because its trees are built independently. Xu and colleagues, the study my design follows most closely, applied transfer learning only to a linear model and XGBoost for the same reason. So I have eight models per analysis instead of nine."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How exactly did you do transfer learning for XGBoost?",
 '"I took the fitted general XGBoost model and continued boosting: extra trees were added, trained only on NCD training patients. The number of extra trees was tuned by cross-validation. So the final model is the general trees plus a set of NCD-specific corrections."',
 "Precise.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How exactly did you do transfer learning for the Cox model?",
 '"The general model\'s linear predictor is used as a fixed offset, and a penalised adjustment is fitted on NCD training patients. The penalty keeps the adjustment small unless the NCD data supports a change, and its strength is tuned by cross-validation. Simply restarting an ordinary Cox fit from the general coefficients would just converge to the disease-specific model."',
 "Precise. The last sentence shows deep understanding.",
 '"The formula is in Chapter 3; may I write it on the board?"'),
("Intermediate", "What is negative transfer?",
 '"Negative transfer is when starting from the general model makes performance worse than training on the target group alone, because the source population differs too much. My design detects it directly: if transfer learning performs worse than the disease-specific model on the held-out group, that is negative transfer."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How did you make sure the transfer learning model never saw test patients?",
 '"The general model is trained only on training data, which excludes the held-out group of NCD patients. During tuning, the general model was refitted without each validation fold, so no validation patient influenced the model being adapted. The test patients are untouched until the final evaluation."',
 "Firm.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "The general model includes NCD training patients. Is the comparison still fair?",
 '"Yes. That is what a general model is in practice: all ICU patients, NCD patients included. What matters is that no model sees the held-out group of NCD patients. The question is whether adding non-NCD patients helps or hurts, and that is exactly what the comparison tests."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How did you tune the random survival forest?",
 '"With randomised search and 5-fold cross-validation on training data, varying the number of trees, the minimum number of patients in a leaf, and the number of predictors tried at each split, and choosing by mean C-index."',
 "Brief.",
 '"The exact grid is in the appendix."'),
("Intermediate", "Which XGBoost survival objective did you use, and why?",
 '"[State: accelerated failure time or Cox.] The accelerated failure time objective models log survival time and gives predicted times directly; the Cox objective gives relative risk like the Cox model. I chose [the one used] because [reason, for example it allows continued boosting for transfer learning and handles censoring natively]."',
 "Precise.",
 '"I would need to check the code to give the exact parameter settings."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you handle tied event times?",
 '"Survival time is measured in days, so ties are common. The Cox implementations handle ties with standard approximations, such as Efron\'s method, and the C-index treats tied pairs according to the usual rules."',
 "Technical.",
 '"I used the library defaults; I can check which tie method was applied."'),
("Intermediate", "Which C-index did you use, Harrell's or Uno's?",
 '"[State which.] Harrell\'s C is the classic version. Uno\'s C uses censoring weights and is less biased when censoring is heavy. With 75% of patients censored, Uno\'s C is a sensible check."',
 "Precise.",
 '"If I reported only one, I would add the other as a second check."'),
("Intermediate", "How did you assess calibration for survival models?",
 '"At fixed time points, such as one year, I grouped patients by predicted risk and compared the predicted probability of death with the Kaplan-Meier estimate in each group, shown as a calibration plot. The integrated Brier score adds an overall measure."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How did you compare the three approaches statistically?",
 '"Every model predicts for the same test patients, so I used a paired bootstrap: 1,000 resamples of the held-out group, computing the difference in C-index and integrated Brier score between approaches in each resample. The 95% interval of the difference tells whether it is significant."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "What did your exploratory analysis of the outcomes show?",
 '"Of all 65,283 patients, 25% died within a year, 18.2% within 90 days and 13.5% within 28 days; 10.8% died in hospital. Among survivors to discharge, 58.8% went home. For NCD patients the figures were 22.8% within a year and 62.9% discharged home."',
 "Numerate and calm.",
 '"The outcome table is in the EDA chapter; may I refer to it?"'),
("Intermediate", "Which predictors had the most missing data, and why?",
 '"Bilirubin was missing for 48.7% of patients, pH for 36.1% and lactate for 31.4%. These tests are ordered when clinically indicated, not routinely. The vital signs and routine blood tests were almost complete, mostly below 3% missing."',
 "Precise.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Why median imputation for Cox and the random survival forest but not for XGBoost?",
 '"Cox cannot fit with missing values, and the random survival forest implementation I used needs complete data. XGBoost learns a default direction for missing values at each split, so imputing would throw away that ability. Missing indicators give the other two models some of the same information."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "Is death a competing risk for discharge home?",
 '"Yes. I defined the secondary outcome only among patients who survived to discharge, so death is excluded rather than modelled. This answers whether a survivor goes home, not the probability of going home from admission. A competing risks analysis would be a reasonable extension."',
 "Honest.",
 '"I chose a simpler definition; competing risks would be the more complete approach."'),
("Intermediate", "Why define discharge home only among survivors?",
 '"Because the question of whether a patient goes home only applies to patients alive at discharge. It also makes the outcome different from death, so it really tests whether the ranking holds for another outcome."',
 "Brief.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How did your earlier binary mortality results inform this study?",
 '"In the legacy analysis, XGBoost had the best AUROC, about 0.91, and the three approaches performed almost the same within each method. That told me the predictors were strong and that differences between approaches might be small, so I needed a careful paired comparison with confidence intervals."',
 "Reflective.",
 '"That work is superseded, so I treat it only as background."'),
("Intermediate", "Which approach performed best for one-year survival?",
 R + ' Structure: "On the held-out group of NCD patients, [approach] had the highest C-index for [methods], [value] with 95% CI [range], compared with [values] for the others. The difference was [significant / not significant]. Calibration was [describe]."',
 "Direct. Lead with the answer, then the numbers.",
 '"May I turn to Table 4.x so I give the exact figures?"'),
("Intermediate", "Were the differences between approaches clinically meaningful?",
 R + ' Structure: "The largest difference in C-index was [value]. In practice, [explain whether that changes risk ranking enough to matter]. Calibration differences were [describe], which matter more for individual estimates."',
 "Balanced.",
 '"Statistically they were [significant / not]; whether that matters clinically would need a decision analysis."'),
("Intermediate", "Did the ranking hold in the sensitivity analysis?",
 R + ' Structure: "Yes / partly. Under the first-three-diagnoses definition, with 40,733 NCD patients, the ranking was [same / different] because [reason]."',
 "Confident.",
 '"The sensitivity results are in Section 4.x; I can show them."'),
("Intermediate", "Did the ranking hold for discharge home alive?",
 R + ' Structure: "For discharge home alive, [approach] performed best, which [agrees / disagrees] with the survival result. This suggests [interpretation]."',
 "Clear.",
 '"May I refer to the secondary outcome table?"'),
("Intermediate", "How did the models perform within each NCD group?",
 R + ' Structure: "Within cardiovascular patients, results were close to the overall NCD result because they dominate the group. For cancer patients, [describe]. The chronic respiratory and diabetes groups had wide intervals, so I draw no firm conclusion for them."',
 "Careful.",
 '"The per-group results are in Table 4.x."'),
("Intermediate", "Which method performed best: Cox, random survival forest or XGBoost?",
 R + ' Structure: "[Method] performed best. This [agrees / contrasts] with Huang and colleagues, who found machine learning no better than Cox for cancer survival, and with Wang and colleagues, where the random survival forest beat Cox for stroke."',
 "Balanced.",
 '"The method ranking was secondary to the approach comparison, but here is what I found ..."'),
("Intermediate", "How does your C-index compare with published work?",
 R + ' Structure: "My best C-index was [value]. Wang and colleagues reported C-indices of about 0.79 on MIMIC-IV for 90-day mortality. Direct comparison is limited because cohorts, horizons and predictors differ."',
 "Measured.",
 '"I would be careful comparing directly, because the populations differ."'),
("Intermediate", "Why do you think transfer learning helped, or did not help?",
 R + ' If it helped: "The general model learnt patterns shared by all ICU patients from more data, and the adjustment corrected where NCD patients differ." If it did not: "NCD patients are already 47% of the cohort, so the general model already learnt them well, leaving little to correct."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"That is my interpretation of the results; testing it would need further experiments."'),
("Intermediate", "Three quarters of patients are censored. Is that a problem?",
 '"Not in itself. Here censoring is administrative at 365 days, not patients dropping out, and survival methods are built for it. The 25% who died still give about 16,000 events in the whole cohort, which is plenty. Heavy censoring does argue for checking Uno\'s C-index alongside Harrell\'s."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Intermediate", "How did you handle patients who died within the first 24 hours?",
 '"They stay in the cohort with whatever data they had before death, and their survival time is short. This is a deliberate choice, but it means the model partly predicts deaths that happen during the prediction window. An alternative is to exclude them and predict from the 24-hour mark."',
 "Honest. This is a known design trade-off.",
 '"I would like to check how many such patients there are; it is a fair point and could be a sensitivity analysis."'),
("Intermediate", "What is time zero in your survival analysis?",
 '"Time zero is ICU admission, the same point at which the 24-hour predictor window starts. Survival time runs from the day of ICU admission to the date of death, or to 365 days for patients not known to have died. The date of death in MIMIC-IV has no time of day, so ICU admission is also taken at day level."',
 "Precise. Name the variable if asked: intime in the icustays table.",
 '"It is ICU admission; the definition is in cohort.py and in Section 3."'),
# ---------------- Advanced
("Advanced", "Predictions are made at 24 hours but follow-up starts at admission. Is that consistent?",
 '"Thank you, that is a sharp point. Strictly, a prediction made at 24 hours should start the clock at 24 hours, which is a landmark analysis, and exclude patients who died before then. Starting at admission includes early deaths, which may inflate performance slightly. The bias affects all approaches equally, so the comparison remains fair, but a landmark sensitivity analysis would settle it."',
 "Thoughtful and non-defensive. Acknowledge it and explain the effect.",
 '"I take the point; a landmark analysis would be the cleanest answer and I can add it."'),
("Advanced", "Treatment decisions such as withdrawal of care affect survival. How does that affect your model?",
 '"The model predicts survival under the care patients actually received, including decisions to limit treatment. If clinicians limit care for patients with a poor outlook, the model learns that, and a new prediction could reinforce it. This is why the model should support, not drive, end-of-life decisions."',
 "Serious.",
 '"MIMIC-IV has limited data on code status in my predictor set, so I cannot adjust for it fully."'),
("Advanced", "Could a survival prediction become a self-fulfilling prophecy?",
 '"Yes, that is a real ethical risk. If a low predicted survival leads to less aggressive care, the patient may die partly because of the prediction. Safeguards are showing uncertainty, using predictions to prompt discussion rather than decide, and monitoring outcomes after deployment."',
 "Serious and reflective.",
 '"It is one of the reasons I frame the work as decision support only."'),
("Advanced", "MIMIC-IV is from a US centre between about 2008 and 2019. Would a model trained there work in a Kenyan ICU?",
 '"Not without validation. Patient age, disease mix, the stage at which patients reach the ICU, available tests and treatments all differ. The model would likely need recalibration at minimum. The design, however, transfers: a Kenyan hospital could run the same comparison, and transfer learning is in fact a way to adapt a model trained elsewhere to local data."',
 "Honest and constructive.",
 '"I have no Kenyan data to test this; it is the most important direction for future work."'),
("Advanced", "What would be needed to validate your approach in a Kenyan hospital?",
 '"Electronic records with the same predictors from the first 24 hours, reliable diagnosis coding, and one-year follow-up for death, ideally linked to the civil registration system. Ethical approval and data governance agreements would come first. Even a few thousand patients would allow recalibration and a transfer learning adaptation."',
 "Practical.",
 '"I would need to learn more about local data systems before giving a firm plan."'),
("Advanced", "Could the general model win simply because it has more data?",
 '"Yes, and that is partly the point: the trade-off between more data and more relevant data is exactly what I test. To separate the two, one could train the general model on a sample the same size as the NCD training set. I did / did not do that [state which]; if not, I would add it as a learning-curve analysis."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"A size-matched comparison would separate the effects; it is a good suggestion."'),
("Advanced", "Is NCD too broad a group for one disease-specific model?",
 '"It is broad: cardiovascular disease, cancer, chronic respiratory disease and diabetes behave differently, as the Kaplan-Meier curves show. I used the WHO grouping because it matches how health systems plan, and because separate groups would be too small for chronic respiratory disease and diabetes. To check the cost of pooling, I report results for each group."',
 "Balanced.",
 '"The per-group results are my answer; separate models are future work."'),
("Advanced", "Why not build four separate disease models instead?",
 '"Two of the four groups, with 464 and 963 patients, are too small for stable survival models with 69 predictors. A pooled NCD model is a fair middle ground. With more data, separate models per group would be the next step, and transfer learning would be the obvious way to build them."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Is continuing to train XGBoost really transfer learning, or just more training?",
 '"It is a simple form of transfer learning: the knowledge in the general model\'s trees is kept, and new trees learn only the corrections the NCD group needs. That is the same mechanism Xu and colleagues used. More advanced forms exist, such as domain adaptation, but this one is transparent and widely used."',
 "Confident but open.",
 '"Definitions vary; I follow the one used in the closest published work."'),
("Advanced", "Could domain adaptation or multi-task learning do better?",
 '"Possibly. Multi-task learning could model all NCD groups and non-NCD patients together with shared and group-specific parts. Domain adaptation could reweight non-NCD patients by similarity. These are more complex and less established for survival data, so I chose the simpler method first."',
 "Engaged.",
 '"I have not tested them, so I would not claim they are better or worse."'),
("Advanced", "How does your study differ from Xu and colleagues (2025)?",
 '"Xu and colleagues compared diagnosis-specific, pooled and transfer learning models on eICU, with a yes-or-no mortality outcome and single diagnoses. My study uses MIMIC-IV, treats NCDs as a combined group, uses a survival outcome with time-to-event metrics, and adds a random survival forest. Their paper is also still a preprint."',
 "Precise.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Pfohl and colleagues found standard training on all data hard to beat. Does that contradict your study?",
 '"It sets a strong prior that the general model will be hard to beat, which my design tests directly. Their methods aimed at worst-case subgroup performance, while transfer learning targets one known group. If my general model performs as well as the others, it agrees with them; if transfer learning wins, it shows targeted adaptation can help where methods aimed at worst-case groups did not. [State which.]"',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"Their setting differs, so I see it as context rather than contradiction."'),
("Advanced", "What if C-index differences are tiny but calibration differs?",
 '"Then calibration should guide the choice, because individual survival estimates depend on it. Two models that rank patients equally well can give very different absolute risks. For NCD patients, a disease-specific or transfer learning model may calibrate better even when ranking is similar, which Xu and colleagues also observed."',
 "Clear.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Is the 28-day AUC dominated by in-hospital deaths?",
 '"Largely, yes. 13.5% died within 28 days and 10.8% died in hospital, so most early deaths happen before discharge. The 28-day AUC therefore mainly reflects acute severity, while the one-year AUC also captures deaths after discharge, which depend more on chronic disease. That is one reason to report several time points."',
 "Numerate.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Why did you exclude diagnosis codes as predictors?",
 '"They are assigned at discharge, so they would not be available at 24 hours. Including them would leak future information and overstate performance. The group label is used only to choose who goes into each model."',
 "Firm.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Would a comorbidity index such as Charlson or Elixhauser improve the model?",
 '"Probably, since comorbidity affects ICU survival, as Simpson and colleagues showed. But in MIMIC-IV these are usually built from discharge codes for the same admission, which would leak information. A version built only from previous admissions could be added without leakage."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"It is a good suggestion for future work, provided it uses only prior information."'),
("Advanced", "Using only the first stay throws away data. Why is that acceptable?",
 '"Later stays would add data but break independence, and a patient on a second admission differs from one on a first. With over 65,000 first stays, the gain from extra stays is small compared with the risk of bias. It also makes the patient-level split simple."',
 "Confident.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "How did you handle the change from ICD-9 to ICD-10 coding?",
 '"MIMIC-IV contains both, because US coding switched in 2015. I mapped each version to the four NCD groups separately using equivalent ranges. Small differences between the two systems could shift a few patients between groups, which the sensitivity analysis partly covers."',
 "Precise.",
 '"The mappings are not perfectly equivalent; I chose the closest standard ranges."'),
("Advanced", "How sensitive are your conclusions to the one-year horizon?",
 '"The 28-day and 90-day AUCs give part of the answer, because they show whether the ranking changes over time. Refitting with a different horizon, such as 90 days, would be a full check. [State whether the ranking was consistent across time points.]"',
 "Balanced.",
 '"I report multiple time points; a full refit at another horizon would be a further check."'),
("Advanced", "Who would use a one-year survival prediction made at 24 hours?",
 '"Clinicians planning care and talking with families about expected outcomes, palliative care teams deciding who to see early, and hospital managers estimating follow-up needs. It is less useful for minute-by-minute ICU decisions, where severity scores and monitoring already serve."',
 "Practical.",
 '"The exact use case would be shaped with clinicians before any deployment."'),
("Advanced", "How did you check that continuous predictors have a linear effect in the Cox model?",
 '"The standard Cox model assumes a linear effect on the log hazard. Checks include martingale residual plots or adding spline terms. [State what you did.] The tree methods do not need this assumption, which is part of why I compare them."',
 "Honest.",
 '"If I did not check this formally, I would say so and note the tree methods as the safeguard."'),
("Advanced", "How do you interpret Cox hazard ratios when missing indicators are included?",
 '"With median imputation plus an indicator, the indicator\'s hazard ratio reflects the risk of not having the test, which often means the clinician was less worried. The predictor\'s own hazard ratio applies to patients with measured values. These coefficients are for prediction, not for causal interpretation."',
 "Precise.",
 '"I would be careful interpreting them individually; they serve prediction."'),
("Advanced", "If the general model wins, are disease-specific models useless?",
 '"No. It would mean that for this population, with these predictors, a general model is enough. Disease-specific models may still help for small, very different groups, or where predictors are disease-specific, such as cancer stage. The conclusion is about this setting."',
 "Balanced.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "What gain would justify building a separate disease-specific model?",
 '"A separate model costs effort to build, validate and maintain. I would want a gain that is both statistically clear and large enough to change decisions, for example better calibration at the risk levels where decisions are made. A C-index gain of a few thousandths would not justify it."',
 "Thoughtful.",
 '"There is no fixed threshold; I would decide it with clinical users."'),
("Advanced", "Would your conclusions change if you used eICU instead of MIMIC-IV?",
 '"eICU covers over 200 US hospitals, so it has more variation between sites. The general model might benefit more from that variety, or transfer learning might help more because patients differ more. That is why eICU validation is my first suggestion for future work."',
 "Open and curious.",
 '"I cannot know without running it, which is exactly the point of external validation."'),
("Advanced", "Did you consider that the NCD group might change because coding practice differs between periods?",
 '"Yes, coding depth can change over time and with the ICD version, which could shift who counts as an NCD patient. The random split spreads periods across training and test data, so it does not bias the comparison between approaches, but it could affect how the results transfer to other periods."',
 "Calm.",
 '"I did not test it by period; a temporal split would answer it."'),
("Advanced", "Could severity scores such as SOFA or SAPS II have been added as predictors?",
 '"They could, but most of their components, such as blood pressure, creatinine, bilirubin and the Glasgow Coma Scale, are already among my predictors in raw form. Adding the scores would mostly repeat information. Using a score as a baseline comparator would be useful to show the gain over current practice."',
 "Reasoned.",
 '"A severity score baseline would be a useful addition to show the improvement over current practice."'),
("Advanced", "How do you justify using one dataset for both choosing the approach and judging it?",
 '"Choices were made with cross-validation inside the training data; judgement was made once on the held-out group of NCD patients. So selection and evaluation are separated. What one dataset cannot show is transportability, which needs external data."',
 "Firm.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "Your study is internal to one hospital. Why should anyone trust the ranking?",
 '"Because the ranking comes from a fair, paired comparison on the same patients, and because I check it across three methods, two NCD definitions and two outcomes. A ranking that holds across all of these is unlikely to be chance. Whether it holds in other hospitals is an open question I state clearly."',
 "Confident and composed.",
 'Short answer.'),
("Advanced", "If you had to defend one design decision above all others, which would it be?",
 '"Testing every model on the same held-out group of NCD patients. It is what makes the study a fair comparison. Without it, differences could come from different test sets rather than from the approaches. Everything else in the design follows from that decision."',
 "Strong, final. Speak slowly.",
 'Rehearse this; it is a natural closing question.'),
]
